Automation, Ownership Diffusion, and the Distribution of Capital Returns: A Unified Framework for the Political Economy of Robotic Development

Ouadi Maakoul

Working Paper, v0.3

---

Abstract

This paper develops the Dynamic Automation-Institution-Capital (DAIC) framework, a unified theoretical model of simultaneous cognitive and robotic automation, household portfolio behavior, institutional adaptation, energy-embodied capital, and the distribution of capital returns. The framework formalizes five objects absent from the existing literature jointly: (i) separate cognitive and robotic task spaces with distinct absorption elasticities, (ii) household portfolio distortion under automation-induced human capital risk, (iii) effective ownership as the conversion parameter from productivity to household wealth, (iv) institutional adaptation as a rate constraint on sustainable capital deepening, and (v) two distinct thresholds—an absorption threshold and a wealth threshold—whose relative position determines whether capital deepening is physically sustainable, financially compensatory, or both. A sixth contribution is added in v0.3: an energy layer that makes the automation frontier explicitly price-dependent, generating a Jevons-style feedback in which energy-efficient autonomy accelerates displacement and tightens the institutional adaptation bound. All propositions are conditional on Assumptions A1–A8. Falsification conditions are specified in the final section.

Keywords: automation, ownership diffusion, capital returns, task displacement, portfolio choice, institutional adaptation, energy intensity, robot economics.

---

1. Introduction

The accumulation of robotic and artificial-intelligence capital raises a distributional question that existing frameworks address only partially. Task-based models of automation (Acemoglu and Restrepo 2019, 2020) explain aggregate labor demand but are silent on who owns capital and how returns are distributed. Household-finance models of human-capital risk (Bodie, Merton, and Samuelson 1992; Gomes, Jermann, and Schmid 2024) explain portfolio reallocation under automation exposure but treat institutional structure as exogenous. Normative arguments for ownership diffusion (Freeman 2018) and political-economy accounts of state-led technology adoption (Chen and Rithmire 2026; Ding 2024) identify institutional determinants without formalizing their rate constraints or their threshold effects.

The DAIC framework integrates these strands. Its central object is the Robotic Development Dividend (RDD), defined as the net change in household wealth induced by robotic capital accumulation. The framework's central prediction is that the sign of the median household's RDD is endogenously determined by the interaction of two thresholds: a physical absorption threshold K_R^{\text{abs}} at which the labor market can no longer reabsorb displaced workers at the reservation wage, and a wealth threshold K_R^{\text{RDD}} at which capital returns no longer compensate the median household for human-capital losses. A high-ownership-diffusion economy can sustain capital deepening beyond K_R^{\text{abs}} because capital returns compensate for unmet absorption. A low-diffusion economy experiences a distributional crisis before the physical limit binds.

1.1 Core Research Questions

1. Under what conditions does robotic capital accumulation increase household wealth for the median household rather than only for capital owners?
2. How does the institutional environment—specifically the efficiency with which capital ownership is converted into household claims—mediate the relationship between automation and distribution?
3. What are the rate constraints on sustainable automation, and how do heterogeneous institutional capacities bind at different stages of development?
4. How does automation-induced human-capital risk affect household portfolio decisions, and what are the aggregate implications for capital supply?
5. How does the energy intensity of robotic capital interact with the automation frontier and the institutional rate constraint?
6. What empirical strategies can distinguish the framework's threshold predictions from smooth-diffusion alternatives?

1.2 Contribution

The DAIC framework makes six contributions. First, it models simultaneous cognitive and robotic automation on separate task spaces, permitting distinct absorption elasticities \rho_C \neq \rho_R. Second, it formalizes household portfolio distortion arising from automation-induced human-capital risk, providing a microfoundation for why automation may fail to generate broad-based wealth gains. Third, it introduces effective ownership \theta^{\text{eff}} = \lambda \theta as the conversion parameter from productivity gains to household wealth, with the conversion efficiency \lambda log-additive in institutional components. Fourth, it formalizes institutional adaptation as a rate constraint on sustainable capital deepening, with all adaptation rates normalized to a common metric. Fifth, it distinguishes the absorption threshold and the wealth threshold, whose relative position determines the qualitative regime. Sixth, and new in this version, it adds an energy layer in which the robotic rental rate depends on energy prices, so that energy efficiency acts as an endogenous accelerator of displacement and therefore tightens the rate constraint—a Jevons-style feedback that bridges the framework to energy-autonomy research.

