2. Literature and Gap

2.1 Task-Based Models

Acemoglu and Restrepo (2019, 2020) model production as a continuum of tasks allocated between labor and capital, with automation expanding the capital-performed set and reinstatement creating new labor-using tasks. Their framework explains aggregate labor demand but treats capital as homogeneous and is silent on ownership. Their preferred calibrations for the task-substitution elasticity \sigma range across regimes; in their 2020 JPE specification \sigma > 1 is required for the displacement effect to be non-degenerate, while other task-based calibrations adopt \sigma < 1. The DAIC framework accommodates both and states the sign restrictions of its comparative statics under each regime explicitly.

2.2 Portfolio Choice with Human Capital

The household-finance literature (Bodie, Merton, and Samuelson 1992; Gomes, Jermann, and Schmid 2024) establishes that the optimal share in risky capital depends on the covariance between human-capital returns and capital returns, scaled by the ratio of human to financial wealth. Gomes et al. (2024) show a significant negative effect of automation exposure on household wealth accumulation, with a portfolio-reallocation channel as a non-trivial component. This is the DAIC framework's strongest empirical anchor: it identifies the mechanism through which automation reduces the demand for automation capital.

2.3 Ownership Diffusion

Freeman (2018) argues normatively that the distributional consequences of automation depend on capital-ownership structure. The DAIC framework formalizes this argument through \theta^{\text{eff}} = \lambda\theta, identifies the threshold \theta^{\text{eff}*} below which median household RDD is negative, and calibrates \lambda from observable institutional components. Contemporary policy developments—employee ownership expansion, public equity funds, sovereign-wealth proposals for AI firms (Sanders 2026; OpenAI 2026; Anthropic 2026)—provide instrument-grade variation for identification.

2.4 Diffusion-Forward Industrial Strategy

Chen and Rithmire (2026) document China's diffusion-forward AI strategy: 1,177 registered generative-AI services between 2023 and 2025, 23.2% state-linked. Ding (2024) argues that diffusion, not innovation, determines national power. The DAIC framework formalizes the rate constraints on diffusion through the adaptation vector and the binding constraint.

2.5 The Gap

No existing framework jointly models (i) separate cognitive and robotic task spaces with distinct absorption elasticities, (ii) portfolio distortion under automation-induced human-capital risk, (iii) effective ownership as a conversion parameter, (iv) institutional adaptation as a rate constraint on capital deepening, (v) two distinct thresholds whose relative position determines the regime, and (vi) energy-embodied capital with price-dependent automation frontiers. The DAIC framework addresses this gap.

