3. Mathematical Foundations

3.1 State Space and Task Environment

Time is discrete, t \in \mathbb{N}. Two normalized task continua:

\Theta_C = [0,1] \quad \text{(cognitive task space)}, \qquad \Theta_R = [0,1] \quad \text{(robotic task space)}.

Tasks are ordered within each space so that a_C(i_C) is non-decreasing in i_C and a_R(i_R) is non-decreasing in i_R. The two orderings are not assumed identical.

Joint task distribution. Let \phi(i_C, i_R) denote the joint density of realized task pairs, with \int_{\Theta_C}\int_{\Theta_R} \phi(i_C, i_R)\, di_R\, di_C = 1. Aggregate output is defined over the product measure induced by \phi.

Task characteristics. A task pair (i_C, i_R) has labor requirement a_L(i_C, i_R), cognitive-capital requirement a_C(i_C), robotic-capital requirement a_R(i_R), and total factor productivity A(i_C, i_R).

Assumption A1 (Separate single-crossing). Within \Theta_C, a_C(i_C)/a_L(i_C, \cdot) is non-decreasing in i_C. Within \Theta_R, a_R(i_R)/a_L(\cdot, i_R) is non-decreasing in i_R. No cross-space ordering is assumed.

Assumption A1bis (Independence of task dimensions). \phi(i_C, i_R) = 1 for all (i_C, i_R) \in \Theta_C \times \Theta_R.

The independence assumption is a normalization: it separates the analysis of cognitive from robotic automation and permits distinct elasticities \rho_C, \rho_R. It is relaxed in the computational implementation where sectoral data permit estimating \phi from occupation-level cross-tabulations.

Labor-requirement composition. The cross-space labor requirement takes a CES form:

a_L(i_C, i_R) = \left[ \omega\, a_L^C(i_C)^{\frac{\eta-1}{\eta}} + (1-\omega)\, a_L^R(i_R)^{\frac{\eta-1}{\eta}} \right]^{\frac{\eta}{\eta-1}},

with \omega \in (0,1), \eta > 0. The parameter \eta is the elasticity of substitution between cognitive and robotic labor requirements. When \eta \to 0, the two dimensions are perfect complements (a task requires both cognitive and physical labor at fixed proportions); when \eta \to \infty, the task can be performed entirely by whichever dimension has the lower labor requirement. For the frontier definitions below, we evaluate at the current frontier of the complementary space, so the two frontiers are recursively determined.

Factor prices. Wage w_t; cognitive-capital rental r_{C,t}; robotic-capital rental r_{R,t}; energy price p_{E,t}.

Capital stocks. Cognitive capital K_{C,t}; robotic capital K_{R,t}.

Automation frontiers (simultaneous determination).

I_{C,t} = \sup\{ i_C \in \Theta_C : a_C(i_C)\, r_{C,t} \le a_L(i_C, I_{R,t})\, w_t \},

I_{R,t} = \sup\{ i_R \in \Theta_R : a_R(i_R)\, r_{R,t} \le a_L(I_{C,t}, i_R)\, w_t \}.

The pair (I_{C,t}, I_{R,t}) solves the fixed-point system. Existence follows from the intermediate value theorem under A1; uniqueness requires the Lipschitz bound \partial a_L/\partial i \cdot \partial i/\partial I bounded away from one, which we state as A1ter.

Assumption A1ter (Frontier Lipschitz regularity). The mapping (I_C, I_R) \mapsto (F_C(I_R), F_R(I_C)), where F_C(I_R) and F_R(I_C) are the right-hand sides of the frontier definitions, is a contraction on [0,1]^2.

Energy-adjusted robotic rental. The robotic rental rate decomposes as

r_{R,t}(i) = \rho_{R,t} + p_{E,t}\, e(i),

where \rho_{R,t} is the pure capital rental, p_{E,t} is the energy price, and e(i) is the task-specific energy intensity of robotic execution (kWh per unit output). This specification introduces an energy channel into the automation frontier: \partial I_{R,t}/\partial p_{E,t} < 0 holding \rho_{R,t} fixed.

Assumption A2 (Cost-minimizing method choice). For each (i_C, i_R),

m(i_C, i_R) = \arg\min\{ a_L\, w_t,\ a_C(i_C)\, r_{C,t},\ a_R(i_R)\, r_{R,t}(i_R) \}.

3.2 Aggregate Production

With task-substitution elasticity \sigma > 0:

Y_t = \left[ \int_{\Theta_C}\int_{\Theta_R} \phi(i_C, i_R)\, y(i_C, i_R)^{\frac{\sigma-1}{\sigma}}\, di_R\, di_C \right]^{\frac{\sigma}{\sigma-1}}.

Two regimes matter for comparative statics. Under \sigma > 1, tasks are gross substitutes and automation displaces labor at the extensive margin. Under \sigma < 1, tasks are complements and productivity gains raise labor demand. The DAIC propositions are stated in forms valid in both regimes with explicit sign conditions.

Displacement.

L_{\text{disp},t} = \int_{\Theta_C}\int_{\Theta_R} a_L(i_C, i_R)\, y(i_C, i_R)\, di_R\, di_C - L_t.

Reinstatement. New labor-using tasks of mass N_t enter each period. Net displacement:

L_{\text{disp},t}^{\text{net}} = (1-\rho_C)\, L_{\text{disp},t}^C + (1-\rho_R)\, L_{\text{disp},t}^R.

The asymmetry \rho_C \neq \rho_R is an empirical hypothesis. Evidence from Autor (2015) and the task-displacement literature suggests \rho_C > \rho_R, consistent with smaller physical-skill mismatch for displaced cognitive workers.

Assumption A2bis (Displacement elasticity). Locally,

\frac{dL_{\text{disp}}}{dK_R} \cdot \frac{K_R}{L_{\text{disp}}} = \varepsilon_R,

with \varepsilon_R \ge 0. The unit-elasticity assumption \varepsilon_R = 1 is not imposed; \varepsilon_R is estimated from data (§12).

3.3 Capital Return Decomposition

Capital returns decompose into a competitive component and a rent component:

r_{K,t} = r_{K,t}^{\text{comp}} + r_{K,t}^{\text{rent}}, \qquad r_{K,t}^{\text{rent}} = \mu_t \cdot r_{K,t}^{\text{comp}},

where \mu_t \ge 0 is the markup rate. Households access the competitive component fully through effective ownership; access to the rent component is attenuated by market power:

\theta_{h,t}^{\text{eff}} r_{K,t} = \theta_{h,t}^{\text{eff}} r_{K,t}^{\text{comp}} + \kappa_{\text{rent}}\, \theta_{h,t}^{\text{eff}} r_{K,t}^{\text{rent}}, \qquad \kappa_{\text{rent}} \in [0,1].

The attenuation \kappa_{\text{rent}} < 1 captures the Open Markets Institute (2026) finding that AI-agent rents accrue to platform shareholders and are not fully distributed even to nominal shareholders.

3.4 Household Portfolio

Assumption A3 (Two-period mean-variance approximation). Household h maximizes

\mathbb{E}_t[r_{W,h}] - \frac{\gamma}{2}\operatorname{Var}_t[r_{W,h}],

where \gamma > 0 is the coefficient of relative risk aversion in the induced local approximation. This is a myopic mean-variance approximation to the CRRA problem; the full Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman solution with path-dependent labor income is developed in Biffis, Gozzi, and Prosdocimi (2020, 2025). The approximation is exact for quadratic utility and first-order accurate for CRRA with small shocks; it is sufficient for the comparative statics derived here.

Wealth evolution (reconciled). Denote financial wealth A_{h,t} and human-capital stock H_{h,t}. Total wealth W_{h,t} = A_{h,t} + H_{h,t}. The financial-wealth transition is

A_{h,t+1} = A_{h,t}\bigl[1 + \alpha_{h,t}\, r_{K,t+1} + (1-\alpha_{h,t})\, r_{f,t+1}\bigr] + Y_{h,t+1}^{\text{flow}} - C_{h,t},

where Y_{h,t+1}^{\text{flow}} is labor income and C_{h,t} is consumption. The human-capital stock transition is a revaluation, not an income flow:

H_{h,t+1} = \sum_{s=t+1}^{\infty} \beta^{s-(t+1)} \mathbb{E}_{t+1}[w_{h,s}(1-u_{h,s})].

The flow change \Delta H_{h,t}^{\text{flow}} and the stock revaluation \Delta H_{h,t} = H_{h,t+1} - H_{h,t} are distinct objects; the former enters the wealth transition, the latter enters the RDD decomposition.

Human capital with persistent displacement. Displaced workers face a Markov transition with reemployment hazard d_h. The expected-discount factor applied to displaced labor income is \beta[(1-u_h) + u_h d_h], which is the dominant eigenvalue of the two-state employment chain. This replaces the level-shift specification of v0.2.

Optimal portfolio share (myopic approximation).

\alpha_{h,t}^* = \frac{\mu_K - r_f}{\gamma\, \sigma_K^2} - \frac{\sigma_{HK,h}}{\sigma_K^2} \cdot \frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}}.

Corner constraints and participation.

\alpha_{h,t}^{\text{obs}} = \min\bigl\{ \max\{ \alpha_{h,t}^*,\, 0 \},\, \alpha_{\max} \bigr\} \cdot \mathbb{1}\{ \text{participate}_{h,t} \},

where participation follows a threshold rule on financial wealth A_{h,t} \ge \underline{A} reflecting fixed participation costs. The extensive margin is essential: 42% of US families hold no stock, and 34% of bottom-half-income families participate versus 95% of the top decile (SCF 2022).

Proposition 1 (Automation-induced portfolio rebalancing). If automation exposure raises \sigma_{H,h}^2 and \sigma_{HK,h}, the household reduces \alpha^* unless the borrowing constraint binds.

Proof. \partial \alpha^*/\partial \sigma_{HK} = -(1/\sigma_K^2)(H/A) < 0. ∎

3.5 Effective Ownership

Definition.

\theta_{h,t}^{\text{eff}} = \lambda_t\, \theta_{h,t},

where \theta_{h,t} is the household's nominal share of aggregate capital, measured as the share of financial wealth excluding human capital, consistent with the Survey of Consumer Finances definition.

Log-additive conversion efficiency.

\log \lambda_t = \log \lambda_{\text{liq}} + \log \lambda_{\text{ctrl}} + \log \lambda_{\text{tax}} + \log \lambda_{\text{trans}} + \log \lambda_{\text{access}} + \varepsilon_{\text{interaction}}.

Two fiscal components replace the conflated \lambda_{\text{fiscal}} of v0.2: \lambda_{\text{tax}} (attenuation from capital taxation) and \lambda_{\text{trans}} (augmentation from net transfers received by the household). The two enter with opposite signs, and the household's net position is

\log \lambda_{\text{fisc},h} = \log \lambda_{\text{tax}} + \log \lambda_{\text{trans},h}.

Extensive-margin handling. The participation component \lambda_{\text{access}} is defined over the participating subpopulation:

\lambda_{\text{access}} = \frac{\sum_{h \in \mathcal{P}} \theta_h}{\sum_{h} \theta_h},

where \mathcal{P} is the participation set. Non-participating households have \theta_h = 0 already; \lambda_{\text{access}} is not applied multiplicatively to their share but enters through the aggregate capital-supply constraint. This resolves the double-counting of the extensive margin in v0.2.

Assumption A4. \lambda_t is increasing in ownership-diffusion institutions: ESOP coverage, public investment funds, universal savings vehicles, and sovereign-equity schemes.

3.6 RDD Conversion Function

Definition.

\text{RDD}_{h,t} \equiv \Delta W_{h,t}.

Decomposition.

\Delta W_{h,t} = \theta_{h,t}^{\text{eff}}\, \Delta V_{K,t} + \Delta H_{h,t},

where \Delta V_{K,t} is the change in the present value of capital returns and \Delta H_{h,t} is the human-capital stock revaluation.

Positivity condition.

\text{RDD}_{h,t} > 0 \iff \theta_{h,t}^{\text{eff}}\, \Delta V_{K,t} > -\Delta H_{h,t}.

Threshold effective ownership.

\theta_{h,t}^{\text{eff}*} = \frac{-\Delta H_{h,t}}{\Delta V_{K,t}}.

Households with \theta^{\text{eff}} > \theta^{\text{eff}*} gain; households below lose. The threshold is household-specific and depends on \lambda through \theta^{\text{eff}} = \lambda\theta.

