4. Absorption and the Two Thresholds

4.1 Sector S Production

Y_{S,t} = A_{S,t}\, L_{S,t}^\beta\, K_{S,t}^{1-\beta}, \qquad \beta \in (0,1).

Wage:

w_{S,t} = \beta\, A_{S,t} \left(\frac{K_{S,t}}{L_{S,t}}\right)^{1-\beta}.

Absorption capacity at the reservation wage \underline{w}_t.

L_S^* = K_{S,t}\left(\frac{\beta\, A_{S,t}}{\underline{w}_t}\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\beta}}.

Assumption A5bis (Bounded sector-S capital). K_{S,t} \le \kappa\, K_{R,t} with \kappa < 1, or K_{S,t} exogenously fixed in the short run.

4.2 Absorption Threshold

Definition. K_R^{\text{abs}} solves

(1-\rho_R)\, L_{\text{disp}}^R(K_R^{\text{abs}}) = L_S^* - L_S^0 - (1-\rho_C)\, L_{\text{disp}}^C.

The absorption threshold contains no \lambda term. It is a purely physical-labor-market object.

4.3 RDD Threshold

Definition. For household h, K_R^{\text{RDD},h} solves

\theta_h^{\text{eff}}\, \Delta V_K(K_R^{\text{RDD},h}) = -\Delta H_h(K_R^{\text{RDD},h}).

The RDD threshold is household-specific and depends on \lambda through \theta_h^{\text{eff}} = \lambda \theta_h.

4.4 Relationship Between the Two Thresholds

A high-\lambda economy satisfies K_R^{\text{RDD}} > K_R^{\text{abs}}: capital returns compensate for unmet absorption at capital stocks below the RDD threshold. A low-\lambda economy satisfies K_R^{\text{RDD}} < K_R^{\text{abs}}: the wealth crisis precedes the physical absorption constraint.

4.5 Proposition 2 (Existence of the absorption threshold, with price feedback)

Statement. Under A1–A5bis, if (i) I_R is non-decreasing in K_R at given (w_t, r_{R,t}), (ii) the wage response to displacement satisfies \partial \ln w/\partial \ln K_R \ge -\underline{\zeta} for some bounded \underline{\zeta} \ge 0, and (iii) L_S^* is bounded above, then there exists K_R^{\text{abs}} < \infty such that for all K_R > K_R^{\text{abs}}, the absorption constraint binds.

Proof sketch. In partial equilibrium, I_R expands with K_R because r_{R,t} falls with K_R and a_R(i_R) r_R crosses a_L w at higher i_R. The general-equilibrium feedback is two-signed: \uparrow K_R \Rightarrow \downarrow r_R \Rightarrow \uparrow I_R and \uparrow K_R \Rightarrow \uparrow L_{\text{disp}} \Rightarrow \downarrow w \Rightarrow \uparrow I_R. Under condition (ii), the wage decline is bounded and does not reverse monotonicity. Since L_{\text{disp}}^R is continuous and increasing and L_S^* - L_S^0 - (1-\rho_C)L_{\text{disp}}^C is bounded above by A5bis, the intermediate value theorem gives a finite crossing. ∎

4.6 Comparative Statics

\frac{\partial K_R^{\text{abs}}}{\partial K_S} > 0, \qquad \frac{\partial K_R^{\text{abs}}}{\partial \underline{w}} < 0, \qquad \frac{\partial K_R^{\text{abs}}}{\partial A_S} > 0,

\frac{\partial K_R^{\text{RDD},h}}{\partial \lambda} > 0, \qquad \frac{\partial K_R^{\text{RDD},h}}{\partial \theta_h} > 0.

The derivative \partial K_R^{\text{RDD}}/\partial \lambda is the formal connection between ownership diffusion and RDD stability. Proof: from \theta_h^{\text{eff}} \Delta V_K(K_R^{\text{RDD}}) = -\Delta H_h(K_R^{\text{RDD}}), with \Delta V_K increasing and -\Delta H_h decreasing in K_R, an increase in \theta_h^{\text{eff}} = \lambda \theta_h requires a higher K_R^{\text{RDD}} to restore equality. ∎

