5. Institutional Adaptation as a Rate Constraint

5.1 The Adaptation Vector in Common Units

The five adaptation channels operate on different physical quantities. To impose a binding constraint, all rates are converted to displaced-worker-equivalents reabsorbed per year:

A_E^{\text{eff}} = A_E \quad (\text{workers/year, direct}),

A_O^{\text{eff}} = A_O^\lambda \cdot L_{\text{disp}} \cdot \psi_O \quad (\text{ownership-induced demand for labor}),

A_F^{\text{eff}} = \frac{A_F^\$ \cdot \mu_F}{\bar{w}} \quad (\text{fiscal transfers converting to wage-equivalents}),

A_S^{\text{eff}} = A_S^{\text{persons}} \cdot \delta_S \quad (\text{insurance-retention effect on search}),

A_C^{\text{eff}} = A_C^{\text{firms}} \cdot \bar{L}_{\text{firm}} \quad (\text{entry expanding labor demand}).

Here A_O^\lambda is the level rate \Delta \lambda per year (not a growth rate), \psi_O converts ownership diffusion into labor demand, \mu_F is the fiscal multiplier on reemployment, \delta_S is the retention rate under insurance expansion, and \bar{L}_{\text{firm}} is average firm employment.

Binding constraint.

A_{\min}(t) = \min\{ A_E^{\text{eff}}, A_O^{\text{eff}}, A_F^{\text{eff}}, A_S^{\text{eff}}, A_C^{\text{eff}} \}.

All terms are in worker-equivalents per year; the minimum is dimensionally coherent.

5.2 λ Transition

\lambda_{t+1} = \lambda_t + A_{O,t}^\lambda\, (1-\lambda_t)\, \Delta t,

where A_{O,t}^\lambda \in [0,1] is the level rate of ownership diffusion per year. The saturation term (1-\lambda_t) ensures \lambda_t \in [0,1]; the logistic form is a reduced-form representation of diffusion dynamics rather than the growth-rate specification of v0.2.

5.3 Sustainable Acceleration Condition

With displacement elasticity \varepsilon_R from A2bis:

\frac{dL_{\text{disp}}}{dt} = \varepsilon_R\, \frac{L_{\text{disp}}}{K_R} \cdot \dot{K}_R = \varepsilon_R\, L_{\text{disp}}\, g_{R,t},

where g_{R,t} \equiv \dot{K}_R/K_R.

Rate constraint.

\varepsilon_R\, L_{\text{disp},t}\, g_{R,t} \le A_{\min}(t) \quad \iff \quad g_{R,t} \le \frac{A_{\min}(t)}{\varepsilon_R\, L_{\text{disp},t}}.

5.4 Proposition 3 (Rate constraint)

Sustainable acceleration of robotic capital is bounded by the ratio of the slowest institutional adaptation rate to displacement, scaled by the displacement elasticity \varepsilon_R. ∎

Corollary 3.1 (Concentration trap). High A_E with low A_O yields rising K_R and falling median RDD.

Corollary 3.2 (Stagnation trap). High A_O with low A_E yields stable RDD only if ownership returns compensate for slow reabsorption.

Corollary 3.3 (Binding-constraint switching). The identity of \arg\min_j A_j changes along the development path. Early-stage robotic industrialization is A_E-constrained; late-stage is A_O-constrained.

