6. Energy Layer and the Jevons Feedback

6.1 Layer 0 Specification

The robotic rental rate is

r_{R,t}(i) = \rho_{R,t} + p_{E,t}\, e(i),

with e(i) > 0 the task-specific energy intensity. The automation frontier shifts with energy price:

\frac{\partial I_{R,t}}{\partial p_{E,t}} = -\frac{e(I_{R,t})}{a_R'(I_{R,t})\, r_{R,t} - a_L'(\cdot)\, w_t} < 0

under the regularity a_R'(I_R) r_R > a_L'(\cdot) w at the frontier.

6.2 Proposition 5 (Jevons feedback in autonomy)

Statement. Under A1–A2bis, an exogenous reduction in energy intensity e(i) for all i shifts I_{R,t} upward, raises dL_{\text{disp}}/dt for given g_{R,t}, and therefore tightens the sustainable-growth bound g_{R,t} \le A_{\min,t}/(\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp},t}).

Proof. Energy efficiency lowers p_E e(i), lowering r_R(i), shifting the crossing of a_R(i_R) r_R(i_R) and a_L(I_C, i_R) w to higher i_R, so I_R rises. Since L_{\text{disp}} is increasing in I_R, L_{\text{disp}} rises at given K_R and t. The rate constraint's denominator \varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp}} rises, tightening the bound. ∎

The proposition states that energy-efficient autonomy is not only an engineering objective but a macroeconomic rate variable: improvements to autonomy's energy efficiency accelerate the required institutional adaptation, a Jevons-style mechanism operating on the displacement margin rather than the consumption margin.

Falsification F8. Energy prices do not shift I_R (test \partial I_R/\partial p_E = 0).

6.3 Inter-Layer Contract

Layer 0 → Layer 1 outputs (p_E, e(i)). Layer 1 → Layer 4 outputs the required adaptation rate, which now depends on energy-driven frontier shifts. The circularity (energy efficiency → frontier → displacement → required adaptation → investment in energy efficiency) is resolved in the dynamical system as a two-time-scale process.

