8. Modular Architecture

The DAIC framework is organized as a six-layer modular architecture:

Layer 0 — Energy. p_{E,t}, e_t(i), \iota_{E,t}. Outputs to Layer 1: energy-adjusted rental rate.

Layer 1 — Task-Capital Production. I_C, I_R, L_{\text{disp}}, Y_t, r_K^{\text{comp}}, r_K^{\text{rent}}.

Layer 2 — Household Portfolio. \alpha_h^{\text{obs}}, \theta_h^{\text{eff}}, \Delta H_h, \text{RDD}_h, K_R^{\text{RDD}}.

Layer 3 — Absorption. L_S^*, K_R^{\text{abs}}, reservation wage floor.

Layer 4 — Institutional Adaptation. \mathcal{A}^{\text{eff}}, A_{\min}, binding identity.

Layer 5 — Governance and Policy Feedback. \tau_t, s_t, \iota_{E,t}, g_{R,t}, responding to RDD distribution and V_t.

8.1 Interface Contracts

From → To Output Input
0 → 1 r_R(i; p_E) Task energy intensities e(i)
1 → 2 L_{\text{disp}} distribution, \Delta V_K, r_K^{\text{comp}}, r_K^{\text{rent}}, \sigma_{HK} Portfolio parameters
2 → 3 Full RDD distribution, \Delta H_h distribution Labor supply of displaced workers
3 → 4 L_{\text{disp}} - L_S^*, K_R^{\text{abs}} Required adaptation rate
4 → 5 \mathcal{A}^{\text{eff}}, A_{\min}, binding identity Policy levers
5 → 1 g_R, \tau, s, \iota_E Capital accumulation, task structure
5 → 0 \iota_E Energy-efficiency investment

Contract note. Layer 2 → Layer 3 must output the full distribution of \Delta H_h, not only the mean, because Layer 3 computes L_S^* for the marginal displaced worker.

