9. Estimation Equations

9.1 E1 — Portfolio Channel

\alpha_{h,t}^{\text{obs}} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \frac{\mu_K - r_f}{\sigma_K^2} + \beta_2 \frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}} + \beta_3 \text{exposure}_{h,t-5} + \beta_4 \mathbb{1}\{A_{h,t} \ge \underline{A}\} + \varepsilon_{h,t}.

Prediction: \beta_3 < 0 for participants; \beta_4 > 0 captures the participation margin.

9.2 E2 — RDD Conversion (Residual Design)

The definitional decomposition \Delta W_h = \theta_h^{\text{eff}} \Delta V_K + \Delta H_h is an identity and cannot be estimated as a regression in levels; coefficients would mechanically equal one. Define the residual

R_{h,t} \equiv \Delta W_{h,t} - \theta_{h,t}\, \Delta V_{K,t} - \Delta H_{h,t}.

Then R_{h,t} captures the deviation from nominal ownership and is the object of interest:

R_{h,t} = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 (1-\lambda_t)\, \theta_{h,t}\, \Delta V_{K,t} + \gamma_2\, \text{exposure}_{h,t-5} + \gamma_3\, \theta_{h,t}\, \Delta V_{K,t}^{\text{rent}} + \varepsilon_{h,t}.

Predictions: \gamma_1 > 0 (the loss from conversion inefficiency is proportional to the nominal ownership share and the size of capital gains); \gamma_2 < 0; \gamma_3 < 0 (rent component is attenuated).

9.3 E3 — Absorption

w_{S,t} = \delta_0 + \delta_1 \log(K_{S,t}/L_{S,t}) + \delta_2 L_{\text{disp},t} + \delta_3 p_{E,t} + \varepsilon_t.

Predictions: \delta_2 < 0; \delta_3 tests the energy channel's pass-through to wages (F8).

9.4 E4 — Rate Constraint

g_{R,t} = \min\left\{ \eta\, \frac{A_{\min,t}}{\varepsilon_R\, L_{\text{disp},t}},\, g_{\max} \right\} + \varepsilon_t.

Prediction: the binding-constraint identity shifts with K_R across stages of development.

9.5 E5 — Threshold Regression

DAIC's central prediction is a regime change, not a smooth decline. Linear regression cannot distinguish "smoothly declining RDD" from "kink at K_R^{\text{RDD}}." Hansen (2017) kink threshold regression is the appropriate estimator:

\Delta W_{h,t} = \begin{cases} \phi_1 X_{h,t} + \varepsilon_{h,t}, & K_{R,t} \le K_R^{\text{RDD}}, \\ \phi_2 X_{h,t} + \varepsilon_{h,t}, & K_{R,t} > K_R^{\text{RDD}}, \end{cases}

with continuity at the kink imposed and only the derivative discontinuous. The economic justification for continuity is that household wealth does not jump when K_R crosses the threshold; what changes is the rate at which wealth responds to further capital deepening. The threshold K_R^{\text{RDD}} is estimated endogenously along with \phi_1, \phi_2. The test H_0: \phi_1 = \phi_2 is the direct test of DAIC against a linear-diffusion model.

