10. Identification Strategy

10.1 Instruments

Instrument 1 — Automation exposure. Shift-share using pre-period task composition \times industry-level robot adoption (Acemoglu–Restrepo 2019), anchored to IFR World Robotics stock (4.66M operational units in 2024, 542,000 new installations, Asia 74% share).

Instrument 2 — Ownership diffusion. Policy discontinuities in ESOP tax treatment, privatization voucher schemes, mandatory profit-sharing thresholds. Anchors: NCEO 2023 ESOP coverage of 15.1M participants, $1.3T in assets; SCF 2022 participation rates (34% bottom-half income, 95% top decile).

Instrument 3 — Institutional adaptation. Exogenous shocks to training capacity (funding discontinuities, program rollouts).

Instrument 4 — Sovereign-equity and public-fund policy discontinuities. The 2026 policy wave provides instrument-grade variation: the American AI Sovereign Wealth Fund Act (one-time 50% equity transfer from major AI firms into a public fund); OpenAI's proposed 5% public equity stake; Anthropic's national-SWF proposal; the Alaska Permanent Fund as the model. These constitute exogenous shifts in \lambda_{\text{ctrl}} and \lambda_{\text{access}}, orthogonal to household-level exposure.

Instrument 5 — Cross-regime variation. Chen and Rithmire (2026) document 1,177 registered generative-AI services in China 2023–25, 23.2% state-linked. The Chinese diffusion-forward model and the US concentrated-private model provide \lambda-variation at similar K_R, the ideal test of the paper's central prediction.

Instrument 6 — Energy-price shocks. Europe's 2022 energy-price shock as an exogenous shift in p_E, testing \partial I_R/\partial p_E < 0 (F8).

10.2 Threats

Reverse causality (wealthier households adopt automation faster); omitted regional conditions; measurement error in IFR stocks; selection into ESOP participation; endogenous location of sovereign-fund investments.

