15. Policy Interpretation

15.1 Constraint Structure

The feasible region is defined by two constraints of different natures:

Supply-side absorption constraint.

g_{R,t} \le \frac{A_{\min,t}}{\varepsilon_R\, L_{\text{disp},t}}.

Demand-side capital-supply constraint.

g_{R,t} \le \frac{\sum_h \alpha_{h,t}^{\text{obs}}\, A_{h,t}}{K_{R,t}} - \delta_R.

The two are not combined by a \min operator. The first is a physical-institutional limit on absorption; the second is a household-portfolio limit on capital supply. Policy sets g_{R,t} subject to both constraints holding simultaneously, and the feasible region is their intersection. The v0.2 formulation collapsed them into a single \min, which implicitly assumed households automatically reduce savings to respect A_{\min}—a non-behavioral assumption.

15.2 Proposition 4 (Optimal policy characterization)

Statement. Under the following sufficient conditions—(i) s_t \mapsto A_{\min,t}(s_t) concave, (ii) cost of s_t convex, (iii) fiscal feasibility set \mathcal{F} non-empty and compact, (iv) transversality \lim_{t\to\infty} e^{-\rho t} \partial V/\partial A_{\min} \cdot A_{\min,t} = 0—the optimal policy maximizing

\int_0^\infty e^{-\rho t}\, \text{RDD}_{\text{median},t}\, dt

subject to the transition equations and the rate constraint is interior: g_{R,t} = A_{\min,t}/(\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp},t}), and s_t is chosen to raise A_{\min} up to the fiscal-feasibility frontier. ∎

Why median, not mean. The threshold \theta^{\text{eff}*} is defined for the median household. Under concentrated ownership the mean is dominated by top-decile gains; the median is the politically relevant object. This is an explicit inequality-aversion objective, not a utilitarian one.

Corollary 4.1. Acceleration and assurance are complementary inputs. Assurance is the rate-determining factor of sustainable acceleration.

15.3 Policy Implications

Sustainable automation requires balanced institutional adaptation. Acceleration without assurance—rapid K_R growth without ownership diffusion—produces a distributional crisis once K_R > K_R^{\text{RDD}}. Assurance without acceleration—stable RDD but foregone productivity—produces stagnation. The conversion-optimizing interior balances both, with the rate of sustainable acceleration set by the binding adaptation constraint A_{\min}.

The 2026 policy environment offers instrument-grade variation: sovereign-equity schemes (US Sanders Act, OpenAI proposal, Anthropic proposal) shift \lambda_{\text{ctrl}} and \lambda_{\text{access}} in ways that a low-\lambda baseline cannot; European energy-price shocks shift p_E and test the energy layer's frontier effects; China's diffusion-forward model provides the cross-regime test at comparable K_R.

---

16. Conclusion

The DAIC framework provides a unified analytical structure for the political economy of automation. Its six contributions—separate task spaces, portfolio distortion, effective ownership as conversion parameter, institutional adaptation as rate constraint, the two-threshold structure, and the energy layer—formalize a system in which the distributional consequences of automation are institutionally contingent rather than technologically determined. The same robotic capital stock can generate broadly shared prosperity or concentrated gains and widespread losses depending on the ownership conversion efficiency \lambda, the adaptation rates \mathcal{A}, and the energy-driven pace of frontier expansion.

The framework's testable core is the threshold regression E5: linear models cannot distinguish smooth diffusion from a regime change at K_R^{\text{RDD}}. The critical falsification F6 distinguishes DAIC from diffusion-forward industrial strategy. The energy-layer falsification F8 tests whether energy prices shift the automation frontier.

Three limitations bound the framework. First, it abstracts from international trade; the cross-regime tests using China data partially remedy this. Second, governance feedbacks are reduced-form through Layer 5; the sign of the RDD-to-voice feedback is empirically open (Lall 2025 finds negative in some European contexts). Third, the frontier fixed point (I_C, I_R) is assumed contractive (A1ter); degenerate cases with multiple equilibria are not analyzed. Future work should relax each.

All propositions are conditional on A1–A8. Falsification conditions are binding. The framework is offered as an auditable hypothesis, not a settled conclusion.

