Appendix B: Assumptions

A1 (Separate single-crossing). Within \Theta_C, a_C(i_C)/a_L(i_C,\cdot) is non-decreasing in i_C. Within \Theta_R, a_R(i_R)/a_L(\cdot,i_R) is non-decreasing in i_R. No cross-space ordering is assumed.

A1bis (Independence of task dimensions). \phi(i_C,i_R)=1 for all (i_C,i_R)\in\Theta_C\times\Theta_R.

A1ter (Frontier Lipschitz regularity). The mapping (I_C,I_R)\mapsto(F_C(I_R),F_R(I_C)), where F_C(I_R) and F_R(I_C) are the right-hand sides of the frontier definitions, is a contraction on [0,1]^2 with modulus L<1.

A2 (Cost-minimizing method choice). For each (i_C,i_R), m(i_C,i_R)=\arg\min\{a_L w_t,\, a_C(i_C)r_{C,t},\, a_R(i_R)r_{R,t}(i_R)\}.

A2bis (Displacement elasticity). Locally, dL_{\text{disp}}/dK_R \cdot K_R/L_{\text{disp}}=\varepsilon_R, with \varepsilon_R\ge 0.

A3 (Two-period mean-variance approximation). Household h maximizes \mathbb{E}_t[r_{W,h}]-(\gamma/2)\operatorname{Var}_t[r_{W,h}].

A4 (Institutional monotonicity). \lambda_t is increasing in ownership-diffusion institutions: ESOP coverage, public investment funds, universal savings vehicles, and sovereign-equity schemes.

A5 (Reservation wage). Sector S hires at reservation wage \underline{w}_t until L_S^* is reached.

A5bis (Bounded sector-S capital). K_{S,t}\le\kappa K_{R,t} with \kappa<1, or K_{S,t} exogenously fixed in the short run.

A6 (Labor market clearing). Labor market clears in equilibrium; no involuntary unemployment beyond transition.

A7 (Incomplete markets). Households cannot fully hedge human-capital risk; \sigma_{HK,h}\neq 0 generically.

A8 (Energy-price exogeneity). p_{E,t} is exogenous to the domestic robotic capital stock in the short run.

