Appendix C: Derivation of the Portfolio Rule

C.1 Setup

Household h has financial wealth A_{h,t} and human-capital stock H_{h,t}. Total wealth is W_{h,t}=A_{h,t}+H_{h,t}. The portfolio share in risky capital is \alpha_{h,t}; the remainder (1-\alpha_{h,t}) is held in the risk-free asset with return r_f.

The portfolio return is

r_{W,h,t+1} = r_f + \alpha_{h,t}(r_{K,t+1}-r_f) + \frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}}r_{H,h,t+1} + \varepsilon_{h,t+1},

where r_{H,h,t+1} is the return on human capital and \varepsilon_{h,t+1} captures idiosyncratic labor-income risk.

C.2 Mean-Variance Objective

Under A3, the household maximizes

\mathbb{E}_t[r_{W,h}] - \frac{\gamma}{2}\operatorname{Var}_t[r_{W,h}].

Let \mu_K=\mathbb{E}_t[r_{K,t+1}], \sigma_K^2=\operatorname{Var}_t[r_{K,t+1}], \sigma_{HK,h}=\operatorname{Cov}_t(r_{K,t+1},r_{H,h,t+1}), and \sigma_H^2=\operatorname{Var}_t(r_{H,h,t+1}).

Then

\mathbb{E}_t[r_{W,h}] = r_f + \alpha_{h,t}(\mu_K-r_f) + \frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}}\mu_H,

and

\operatorname{Var}_t[r_{W,h}] = \alpha_{h,t}^2\sigma_K^2 + 2\alpha_{h,t}\frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}}\sigma_{HK,h} + \left(\frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}}\right)^2\sigma_H^2.

C.3 First-Order Condition

Differentiating with respect to \alpha_{h,t}:

(\mu_K-r_f) - \gamma\left[\alpha_{h,t}\sigma_K^2 + \frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}}\sigma_{HK,h}\right] = 0.

Solving:

\alpha_{h,t}^* = \frac{\mu_K-r_f}{\gamma\sigma_K^2} - \frac{\sigma_{HK,h}}{\sigma_K^2}\cdot\frac{H_{h,t}}{A_{h,t}}.

The first term is the myopic demand; the second is the hedging demand. When \sigma_{HK,h}>0, human capital and capital returns are positively correlated, so capital is not a hedge and the household reduces its position. This is the Gomes et al. (2024) channel formalized as the second term.

C.4 Corner Constraints and Participation

The observed portfolio share is

\alpha_{h,t}^{\text{obs}} = \min\{\max\{\alpha_{h,t}^*,0\},\,\alpha_{\max}\}\cdot\mathbb{1}\{A_{h,t}\ge\underline{A}\},

where \underline{A} is a fixed participation cost threshold. Empirical estimates suggest a per-period participation cost of approximately $50 is sufficient to explain non-participation for half of non-participants. The SCF 2022 documents that 42% of US families hold no stock, with participation rates of 34% in the bottom half of the income distribution versus 95% in the top decile.

C.5 Comparative Statics

\frac{\partial\alpha^*}{\partial\sigma_{HK}} = -\frac{1}{\sigma_K^2}\cdot\frac{H}{A} < 0,

\frac{\partial\alpha^*}{\partial(H/A)} = -\frac{\sigma_{HK}}{\sigma_K^2} < 0 \quad\text{when }\sigma_{HK}>0.

Proposition 1 (Automation-induced portfolio rebalancing). If automation exposure raises \sigma_{H,h}^2 and \sigma_{HK,h}, the household reduces \alpha^* unless the borrowing constraint binds. ∎

