Appendix D: Frontier Fixed-Point Existence and Uniqueness

D.1 The Frontier System

The cognitive and robotic frontiers solve simultaneously:

I_{C,t} = F_C(I_{R,t}) = \sup\{i_C\in\Theta_C: a_C(i_C)r_{C,t}\le a_L(i_C,I_{R,t})w_t\},

I_{R,t} = F_R(I_{C,t}) = \sup\{i_R\in\Theta_R: a_R(i_R)r_{R,t}(i_R)\le a_L(I_{C,t},i_R)w_t\}.

D.2 Existence

Proposition D.1. Under A1, there exists at least one fixed point (I_{C,t}^*,I_{R,t}^*)\in[0,1]^2 solving the frontier system.

Proof. The mapping F=(F_C,F_R):[0,1]^2\to[0,1]^2 is continuous under A1 (the single-crossing condition ensures the supremum is attained and varies continuously with the other frontier). By Brouwer's fixed-point theorem, every continuous map from a compact convex set to itself has a fixed point. ∎

D.3 Uniqueness

Proposition D.2. Under A1 and A1ter, the fixed point is unique.

Proof. By A1ter, F is a contraction with modulus L<1. By the Banach fixed-point theorem, a contraction on a complete metric space has a unique fixed point. ∎

D.4 Iterative Solution

The computational implementation iterates

I_{C,t}^{(n+1)} = F_C(I_{R,t}^{(n)}), \qquad I_{R,t}^{(n+1)} = F_R(I_{C,t}^{(n)}),

until \max\{|I_{C,t}^{(n+1)}-I_{C,t}^{(n)}|,|I_{R,t}^{(n+1)}-I_{R,t}^{(n)}|\}<\epsilon. Under A1ter, convergence is geometric with rate L.

