Appendix E: Proofs of Propositions

E.1 Proposition 2 (Existence of the Absorption Threshold)

Statement. Under A1–A5bis, if (i) I_R is non-decreasing in K_R at given (w_t,r_{R,t}), (ii) the wage response to displacement satisfies \partial\ln w/\partial\ln K_R\ge-\underline{\zeta} for some bounded \underline{\zeta}\ge 0, and (iii) L_S^* is bounded above, then there exists K_R^{\text{abs}}<\infty such that for all K_R>K_R^{\text{abs}}, the absorption constraint binds.

Proof. In partial equilibrium, I_R expands with K_R because \partial r_{R,t}/\partial K_R<0 (diminishing marginal product) and a_R(i_R)r_R crosses a_L w at higher i_R. The general-equilibrium feedback is two-signed: \uparrow K_R\Rightarrow\downarrow r_R\Rightarrow\uparrow I_R and \uparrow K_R\Rightarrow\uparrow L_{\text{disp}}\Rightarrow\downarrow w\Rightarrow\uparrow I_R. Under condition (ii), the wage decline is bounded and does not reverse monotonicity. Since L_{\text{disp}}^R(K_R) is continuous and increasing in K_R, and L_S^*-L_S^0-(1-\rho_C)L_{\text{disp}}^C is bounded above by A5bis, the intermediate value theorem gives a finite crossing K_R^{\text{abs}} where

(1-\rho_R)L_{\text{disp}}^R(K_R^{\text{abs}}) = L_S^*-L_S^0-(1-\rho_C)L_{\text{disp}}^C.

For all K_R>K_R^{\text{abs}}, the left-hand side exceeds the right-hand side, so the constraint binds. ∎

E.2 Proposition 3 (Rate Constraint)

Statement. Sustainable acceleration of robotic capital is bounded by

g_{R,t}\le\frac{A_{\min,t}}{\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp},t}}.

Proof. By A2bis, dL_{\text{disp}}/dt=\varepsilon_R(L_{\text{disp}}/K_R)\dot{K}_R=\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp}}g_{R,t}. The adaptation capacity A_{\min,t} is the maximum rate at which displaced workers can be reabsorbed (in worker-equivalents per year). Sustainability requires dL_{\text{disp}}/dt\le A_{\min,t}. Dividing both sides by \varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp},t} gives the bound. ∎

Corollary 3.1 (Concentration trap). If A_E is high and A_O is low, then A_{\min}=A_O binds. K_R can grow rapidly while g_R is constrained by the slow ownership-diffusion rate, so K_R rises but median RDD falls because \theta^{\text{eff}}=\lambda\theta remains low. ∎

Corollary 3.2 (Stagnation trap). If A_O is high and A_E is low, then A_{\min}=A_E binds. g_R is constrained by slow reabsorption. Median RDD is stable only if ownership returns compensate for the limited reabsorption of displaced workers. ∎

Corollary 3.3 (Binding-constraint switching). The identity of \arg\min_j A_j changes along the development path. Early-stage robotic industrialization is A_E-constrained because education and retraining capacity are the bottleneck. Late-stage is A_O-constrained because the marginal displaced worker is absorbed, but ownership concentration limits the distribution of returns. ∎

E.3 Proposition 4 (Optimal Policy Characterization)

Statement. Under (i) s_t\mapsto A_{\min,t}(s_t) concave, (ii) cost of s_t convex, (iii) fiscal feasibility set \mathcal{F} non-empty and compact, and (iv) transversality \lim_{t\to\infty}e^{-\rho t}(\partial V/\partial A_{\min})A_{\min,t}=0, the optimal policy maximizing

\int_0^\infty e^{-\rho t}\text{RDD}_{\text{median},t}\,dt

subject to the transition equations and the rate constraint is interior: g_{R,t}=A_{\min,t}/(\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp},t}), and s_t is chosen to raise A_{\min} up to the fiscal-feasibility frontier.

Proof sketch. The Hamiltonian is

\mathcal{H} = e^{-\rho t}\text{RDD}_{\text{median},t} + \mu_{K_R}[(1-\delta_R+g_R)K_R] + \mu_{\lambda}[\lambda+A_O(1-\lambda)] + \mu_{A}A_{\min}(s_t) + \nu[A_{\min}/(\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp}})-g_R].

The first-order condition for g_R gives \mu_{K_R}=\nu. The first-order condition for s_t gives \mu_A A_{\min}'(s_t)=\partial(\text{cost})/\partial s_t. Interiority follows from concavity of A_{\min} in s and convexity of the cost function. The transversality condition rules out boundary solutions at infinity. ∎

E.4 Proposition 5 (Jevons Feedback in Autonomy)

Statement. Under A1–A2bis, an exogenous reduction in energy intensity e(i) shifts I_{R,t} upward, raises dL_{\text{disp}}/dt for given g_{R,t}, and therefore tightens the sustainable-growth bound g_{R,t}\le A_{\min,t}/(\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp},t}).

Proof. Energy efficiency lowers p_E e(i), lowering r_R(i)= \rho_R+p_E e(i), shifting the crossing of a_R(i_R)r_R(i_R) and a_L(I_C,i_R)w to higher i_R. The derivative

\frac{\partial I_{R,t}}{\partial p_E} = -\frac{e(I_{R,t})}{a_R'(I_{R,t})r_{R,t}-a_L'(\cdot)w_t}<0

under the regularity a_R'(I_R)r_R>a_L'(\cdot)w at the frontier. Since L_{\text{disp}} is increasing in I_R, L_{\text{disp}} rises at given K_R and t. The rate constraint's denominator \varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp}} rises, tightening the bound g_{R,t}\le A_{\min,t}/(\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp},t}). ∎

Remark. This is a Jevons-style mechanism on the displacement margin, not the consumption margin. Energy-efficient autonomy does not increase energy consumption; it increases the rate of labor displacement per unit of robotic capital, thereby tightening the institutional adaptation constraint. The feedback loop—energy efficiency \to frontier \to displacement \to required adaptation \to investment in energy efficiency—is a two-time-scale process resolved in the dynamical system.

E.5 Comparative Statics of the RDD Threshold

Statement. \partial K_R^{\text{RDD}}/\partial\lambda>0.

Proof. From the defining equation \theta_h^{\text{eff}}\Delta V_K(K_R^{\text{RDD}})=-\Delta H_h(K_R^{\text{RDD}}), with \Delta V_K increasing in K_R (capital returns rise with capital deepening) and -\Delta H_h decreasing in K_R (human-capital losses rise with displacement), an increase in \theta_h^{\text{eff}}=\lambda\theta_h requires a higher K_R^{\text{RDD}} to restore equality. ∎

