Appendix H: Hansen Kink Threshold Regression — Implementation Details

H.1 Model

The kink threshold regression model of Hansen (2017) is

Y_i = \begin{cases}
\beta_1'X_i + \varepsilon_i, & q_i\le\gamma,\\
\beta_2'X_i + \varepsilon_i, & q_i>\gamma,
\end{cases}

with continuity at the threshold \gamma:

\beta_1'\mathbb{E}[X_i|q_i=\gamma] = \beta_2'\mathbb{E}[X_i|q_i=\gamma].

Only the derivative is discontinuous. The threshold \gamma is estimated endogenously along with \beta_1,\beta_2. Hansen (2017) provides the asymptotic distribution of the kink estimator and a bootstrap test for the null of no kink.

H.2 Economic Justification for Continuity

Household wealth does not jump when K_R crosses K_R^{\text{RDD}}. What changes is the rate at which wealth responds to further capital deepening. Before the threshold, \partial\Delta W/\partial K_R>0 (capital returns dominate). After the threshold, \partial\Delta W/\partial K_R<0 (human-capital losses dominate). Continuity at the kink is the economically correct restriction: wealth is a state variable and does not admit discontinuous jumps in the deterministic limit.

H.3 Estimation Procedure

1. Grid search over \gamma\in[\underline{\gamma},\overline{\gamma}] (trimmed 5% each tail).
2. For each \gamma, estimate \beta_1,\beta_2 by constrained least squares with the continuity restriction.
3. Select \hat{\gamma} minimizing the sum of squared residuals.
4. Construct bootstrap p-values for the test H_0:\beta_1=\beta_2 using the Hansen (2017) procedure.

H.4 Identification

The threshold variable K_{R,t} is instrumented by shift-share exposure (Acemoglu–Restrepo 2019) to address endogeneity of robot adoption. The continuity assumption is testable using the Seo–Hidalgo continuity test, which nests both continuous and discontinuous specifications.

