Appendix J: Energy Layer — Derivations and Calibration

J.1 Robotic Rental with Energy

r_{R,t}(i) = \rho_{R,t} + p_{E,t}e(i).

The energy term p_E e(i) is the flow cost of energy per unit of robotic output. The task-specific energy intensity e(i) is estimated from robot power-consumption data by task class.

J.2 Frontier Shift

\frac{\partial I_{R,t}}{\partial p_{E,t}} = -\frac{e(I_{R,t})}{a_R'(I_{R,t})r_{R,t}-a_L'(\cdot)w_t}.

The denominator is positive at the frontier under the regularity a_R'(I_R)r_R>a_L'(\cdot)w. The frontier shifts inward when energy prices rise.

J.3 Jevons Feedback

The feedback loop is:

\iota_{E,t}\uparrow \Rightarrow e(i)\downarrow \Rightarrow I_{R,t}\uparrow \Rightarrow L_{\text{disp}}\uparrow \Rightarrow \frac{A_{\min}}{\varepsilon_R L_{\text{disp}}}\downarrow \Rightarrow g_{R,t}\text{ bound tightens.}

This is a genuine feedback mechanism: energy-efficiency investment, intended to reduce the cost of autonomy, accelerates the rate at which autonomy displaces labor, thereby tightening the institutional adaptation constraint. The policy implication is that energy-efficiency R&D and institutional adaptation investment are complementary, not substitutes.

