Appendix O: Proof of the Residual RDD Estimator

O.1 The Identity Problem

The definitional decomposition \Delta W_h=\theta_h^{\text{eff}}\Delta V_K+\Delta H_h is an identity. Regressing \Delta W_h on \theta_h\Delta V_K and \Delta H_h mechanically yields coefficients of one (up to measurement error), so the null \gamma_1=0 can almost never be rejected.

O.2 Residual Design

Define the residual

R_{h,t}\equiv\Delta W_{h,t}-\theta_{h,t}\Delta V_{K,t}-\Delta H_{h,t}.

Under the null that nominal ownership equals effective ownership (\lambda=1), R_{h,t}=0. Under the alternative that \lambda<1, R_{h,t}<0 for households with positive \theta_h\Delta V_{K,t}.

O.3 Estimation Equation

R_{h,t}=\gamma_0+\gamma_1(1-\lambda_t)\theta_{h,t}\Delta V_{K,t}+\gamma_2\text{exposure}_{h,t-5}+\gamma_3\theta_{h,t}\Delta V_{K,t}^{\text{rent}}+\varepsilon_{h,t}.

Predictions: \gamma_1>0 (the loss from conversion inefficiency is proportional to the nominal ownership share and the size of capital gains); \gamma_2<0; \gamma_3<0 (rent component is attenuated).

O.4 Identification

The interaction (1-\lambda_t)\theta_{h,t}\Delta V_{K,t} is instrumented by ESOP policy discontinuities (Instrument 2) and sovereign-fund announcements (Instrument 4), which shift \lambda without directly affecting household wealth. The rent component \theta_{h,t}\Delta V_{K,t}^{\text{rent}} is instrumented by firm-level markup measures (Lerner indices, De Loecker–Eeckhout markups).

O.5 Falsification

F1 tests \gamma_1=0. Rejection of \gamma_1=0 supports the DAIC conversion-inefficiency channel. Failure to reject means nominal ownership translates fully into effective ownership, and the framework collapses to the Freeman (2018) benchmark.

